// @flag display_errors 0|1 default=0 1 prints warnings, notices and deprecations on stdout as php-cli does, honouring @ and error_reporting(); 0 detects none and costs nothing.
// @flag lines 1|0 default=1 With display_errors=1, 0 drops each diagnostic's " in <file> on line <n>" for smaller and faster code.

:title("PHP (subset) interpreter") ;
:description("Parses PHP and executes it directly, without generating code.

A file starts in HTML MODE: text outside '<?php ... ?>' is literal output, '?>' also
stands in for a missing ';', the newline right after a close tag is swallowed and
'<?= e ?>' is shorthand for echo - so a template that interleaves markup with
foreach/endforeach runs as written. Covered:
dollar-sigil variables and variable variables, the full assignment set (= += -= *= /=
%= .= **= &= |= ^= <<= >>= ??=) and reference binding ($b = &$a), all the number
literal forms (decimal, 0x, 0b, 0o, legacy octal, underscores, floats with an
exponent, leading and trailing dot), single and double quoted strings with PHP's
escape set (including octal, hex and Unicode) and interpolation of '$var',
'$var[key]', '$var->prop' and '{$expr}', heredocs and nowdocs with terminator-indent
stripping, arrays as ordered hash maps with spreads, destructuring (nested, keyed
and with holes), isset/empty/unset, the nullsafe '?->' with argument short-circuit,
match expressions, closures with 'use' by value and by reference, arrow functions,
named arguments, variadics, first-class callable syntax, default parameter values,
by-reference parameters, foreach by value, by key and by reference, if/elseif/else,
while, do-while, for, foreach, try/catch/finally, throw as a statement and as an
expression, functions and classes with methods and properties, PHP's array and string
library (lib/php-stdlib.php, written in PHP on a handful of natives), and echo/print.

The value model is PHP's own: int and float are distinct (3.0 === 3 is false), a PHP
array is ONE ordered key/value map (so === compares key order and == does not),
strings are byte strings (strlen counts UTF-8 bytes), '/' yields an int only when the
division is exact, '.' concatenates, and ==/===/<=>/< follow the PHP 8 comparison
rules (0 == \"a\" is false).

The int is 64 bit two's complement (PHP_INT_MAX is 9223372036854775807), and it does
NOT wrap on overflow: an arithmetic result that leaves 64 bits silently becomes a
float, so PHP_INT_MAX + 1 is 9.2233720368548E+18. The bitwise operators DO wrap, an
out-of-range float casts to int modulo 2^64, and an integer literal too large for 64
bits is a float. Floats have TWO renderers, which is PHP's own confusing pair: echo /
(string) / interpolation use precision=14, var_dump uses serialize_precision=-1 (the
shortest round-tripping digits) - so the same value prints as 9.2233720368548E+18 and
9.223372036854776E+18. Division by zero, modulo by zero and a negative shift count are
the catchable DivisionByZeroError / ArithmeticError PHP 8 made them.

Classes are covered in full: inheritance and interfaces, class constants (typed and
self-referential), static properties and methods, self:: / static:: / parent:: with
late static binding, constructor property promotion, anonymous classes, traits with
insteadof and as, enums (pure and backed, with cases()/from()/tryFrom()), the magic
methods (__get __set __isset __unset __call __callStatic __invoke __toString __clone), and
Foo::class. Also covered: switch with fallthrough, do-while, the alternative
colon/endXXX control syntax, break N / continue N, goto within a statement list,
static local variables, global constants and the magic __FUNCTION__ / __CLASS__.

Generators are covered. There are no coroutines in this value model, so a generator
cannot be SUSPENDED, and the body is read two ways instead. foreach (and yield from
over a generator) PULLS: the body runs with the consumer attached as a sink, so each
yielded pair is handed over as it is produced and a break aborts the generator - an
unbounded generator is iterable. Everything that needs a length instead - the
Generator methods, iterator_to_array - COLLECTS, running the body to completion; that
one still cannot terminate on an unbounded generator, and neither can PHP. Creation
stays lazy either way and every side effect happens exactly once, in order. Only
send(), which has to resume a suspended body with a value, is out of reach.

Warnings, notices and deprecations are raised only with the compiler flag
display_errors=1, and displayed on stdout as php-cli does; error_reporting(), '@' and
error_get_last() see them (the flags are in the leading comment block).

The program runs top to bottom; a self checking program ends with exit(failures).") ;


// --- main rules

// Shared token productions (languages/lib): identifiers, integers, booleans.
:include("lib/ident.abnf") ;
:include("lib/numbers.abnf") ;
:include("lib/bools.abnf") ;

:startRule(Program) ;
:whitespace(Whitespace) ;

// PHP whitespace: blanks plus '#' and '//' line comments and '/* */' block comments.
Whitespace  = { @+" \t\r\n" | LineComment | BlockComment } ;
// '#' starts a line comment, but '#[' opens an ATTRIBUTE - the negative lookahead
// keeps the attribute out of the comment.
LineComment = ( "//" | HashOnly ) :whitespace() { NotNewline } :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
HashOnly    = "#" !"[" ;
BlockComment = "/*" :whitespace() { NotStar | "*" { "*" } NotStarSlash } "*" { "*" } "/" :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
NotNewline  = "\x00"..."\x09" | "\x0b"..."\U0010ffff" ;
NotStar     = "\x00"..."\x29" | "\x2b"..."\U0010ffff" ;
NotStarSlash = "\x00"..."\x29" | "\x2b"..."\x2e" | "\x30"..."\U0010ffff" ;

// The whole program. A PHP file starts in HTML mode: everything before the first
// '<?php' is literal output, '?>' switches back to HTML mode, and the file may
// alternate any number of times (a file with no '<?php' at all is pure output).
Program     <~~ runProgram(takeAll()) ~~>
            = LeadHtml { TopItem } ;
// One item of a top level: a declaration or a statement. Factored out because a
// BRACED namespace body holds exactly the same items (see NsDecl).
TopItem     = HaltTail | Def <~~ push(makeTopDef(pop())) ~~> | ClassDecl <~~ push(makeTopClass(pop())) ~~> | EnumDecl
            | ConstDecl | TypeDecl <~~ push(makeTopClass(pop())) ~~> | NsDecl | UseDecl | Statement ;
// __halt_compiler(); ends the program TEXT: whatever follows it is data (read back
// through __COMPILER_HALT_OFFSET__), never parsed. Nothing is pushed, so the
// program simply ends there.
HaltTail    = ( "__halt_compiler" | "__HALT_COMPILER" ) "(" ")" ( ";" | "?>" )
              :whitespace() { "\x00"..."\U0010ffff" } :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
OpenTag     = "<?" :whitespace() CiP CiH CiP :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
// Text before the first open tag. Unlike the '?>' form it keeps a leading newline:
// only the newline directly after a CLOSE tag is swallowed.
LeadHtml    <~~ push(makeHtmlOut(pop(), false)) ~~>
            = HtmlText [ OpenTag ] ;
// '?>' leaves PHP mode. Everything up to the next '<?php' (or end of file) is echoed
// verbatim, and the newline immediately after '?>' is swallowed - PHP's own rule, so
// that a template line does not gain a blank line per tag. It is a STATEMENT, which
// is what makes the interleaved template form work inside a body:
//     <?php foreach ($rows as $r): ?><li><?php echo $r; ?></li><?php endforeach; ?>
// '?>' also stands in for a missing ';' (see Semi), exactly as in PHP.
InlineHtml  <~~ push(makeHtmlOut(pop(), true)) ~~>
            = "?>" HtmlText [ OpenTag ] ;
// The raw run itself. Whitespace skipping is turned OFF around the scan: the leading
// blanks and newlines of the run are OUTPUT, so they must not be eaten as separators
// before the token the scanner returns is matched.
HtmlText    <~~ push(up.in) ~~>
            = :whitespace() HtmlScan :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
HtmlScan = :script(~~
    (function() {
        // Everything from here to the next opening tag is literal text. '<?php' is
        // matched case-insensitively (PHP's tag is), and '<?=' is the echo shorthand,
        // which ShortEcho then parses as an echo statement.
        var end = 0
        var steps = 0
        while (steps < 100000000) {
            steps = steps + 1
            var b = c.peek(end)
            if (b < 0) { break }
            if (b === 60 && c.peek(end + 1) === 63) {
                if (c.peek(end + 2) === 61) { break }
                var p1 = c.peek(end + 2)
                var h1 = c.peek(end + 3)
                var p2 = c.peek(end + 4)
                if ((p1 === 112 || p1 === 80) && (h1 === 104 || h1 === 72) && (p2 === 112 || p2 === 80)) { break }
            }
            end = end + 1
        }
        // An empty run must SUCCEED matching nothing; returning a zero-length token
        // would not. undefined is the dialect's "match empty".
        if (end === 0) { return undefined }
        // c.peek answers BYTES, so the UTF-8 has to be decoded here or a run holding
        // any non-ASCII text would never match back against the source.
        var out = ""
        var p = 0
        while (p < end) {
            var b0 = c.peek(p)
            if (b0 < 128) { out = out + String.fromCharCode(b0); p = p + 1; continue }
            if (b0 >= 240) {
                var cp4 = ((b0 - 240) * 262144) + ((c.peek(p + 1) - 128) * 4096) + ((c.peek(p + 2) - 128) * 64) + (c.peek(p + 3) - 128)
                var off = cp4 - 65536
                out = out + String.fromCharCode(55296 + ((off - (off % 1024)) / 1024)) + String.fromCharCode(56320 + (off % 1024))
                p = p + 4
                continue
            }
            if (b0 >= 224) {
                out = out + String.fromCharCode(((b0 - 224) * 4096) + ((c.peek(p + 1) - 128) * 64) + (c.peek(p + 2) - 128))
                p = p + 3
                continue
            }
            if (b0 >= 192) {
                out = out + String.fromCharCode(((b0 - 192) * 64) + (c.peek(p + 1) - 128))
                p = p + 2
                continue
            }
            return abnf.newToken("\x01badutf8", 0)
        }
        return abnf.newToken(out, 0)
    })()
~~) ;
// '<?= e, e ?>' is shorthand for 'echo e, e;'.
ShortEcho   <~~ push(makeEcho(takeAll(), up.start)) ~~>
            = ShortOpen <~~ push({cmd: "echo"}) ~~> [ Expression { "," Expression } ] [ ";" ] ;
ShortOpen   = "<?=" :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
// A statement terminator. PHP lets a close tag stand in for the ';' ("echo 1 ?>"),
// so every statement that ends in ';' accepts a '?>' AHEAD of it instead - the tag
// itself is left in place and read as the InlineHtml statement that follows.
Semi        = ";" | CloseAhead ;
CloseAhead  = :script(~~
    (function() {
        var i = 0
        var cc = c.peek(i)
        var steps = 0
        while (steps < 100000) {
            steps = steps + 1
            if (cc === 32 || cc === 9 || cc === 13 || cc === 10) { i = i + 1; cc = c.peek(i); continue }
            break
        }
        if (cc === 63 && c.peek(i + 1) === 62) { return undefined }
        return abnf.newToken("\x01noclosetag", 0)
    })()
~~) ;

// A function definition: function name($a, $b) { ... } with explicit return. The
// parameter and return types are checked at the call and at each return.
// 'function &name()' returns by REFERENCE (DefAmp, refFnEnter).
Def         <~~ push(makeFun(takeAll())); refFnStack.pop() ~~>
            = [ AttrGrp ] KwFunction [ DefAmp ] Name <~~ push(up.in); refFnEnter(up.in); genPush(); fnEnter(); cloEnterFn(up.in, false) ~~> ParamList [ ReturnType ] Block ;
DefAmp      <~~ defAmp = true ~~> = "&" ;
ParamList   = "(" [ Param { "," Param } [ "," ] ] ")" ;
// A parameter accepts real-world surface: promotion modifiers (public/readonly...),
// a type, by-reference '&', variadic '...', and a default value; makeParam folds
// them into one record. A default spelled null makes the type nullable.
Param       <~~ push({param: makeParam(takeAll())}) ~~>
            = [ AttrGrp ] { PropMod } [ ParamType ] [ PByRef ] [ PVariadic ] DollarName [ ParamDefault ] ;
ParamType   <~~ push({ptype: propTypeText(up.in)}) ~~> = TypeHint ;
PByRef      <~~ push({pbyref: true}) ~~> = "&" ;
PVariadic   <~~ push({pvariadic: true}) ~~> = "..." ;
ParamDefault <~~ push({pdefault: pop(), pnull: isNullDefault(up.in)}) ~~> = PlainEq Expression ;
TypeHint    = [ "?" ] TypeAtom { ( "|" | "&" ) [ "?" ] TypeAtom } ;
// A DNF type (PHP 8.2) parenthesizes an INTERSECTION inside a union: '(X&Y)|Z',
// 'X|(A&B)'. The parenthesized group is just another atom of the surrounding
// TypeHint, so one alternative covers every position a type may stand in (parameter,
// return, property).
TypeAtom    = KwArray | TypeParen | [ "\\" ] Id { "\\" Id } ;
TypeParen   = "(" TypeHint ")" ;
ReturnType  <~~ push({rtype: propTypeText(up.in.substring(1))}) ~~> = ":" TypeHint ;

// class C { public function m() {...} } -- method modifiers are accepted and ignored.
ClassDecl   <~~ push(makeClassStmt(takeAll())) ~~>
            = [ AttrGrp ] { ClassMod } KwClass Name <~~ push(cloNoteClass(up.in)) ~~> [ KwExtends ExtBase ] [ KwImplements ImplList ] "{" { Member } "}" ;
ExtBase     <~~ push({ext: lastSeg(stripWs(up.in)), raw: stripWs(up.in)}) ~~> = ExtName ;
ClassMod    = ( CiAbstract | CiFinal | CiReadonly ) KwEnd ;
CiAbstract  = CiA :whitespace() CiB CiS CiT CiR CiA CiC CiT :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
CiFinal     = CiF :whitespace() CiI CiN CiA CiL :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
CiReadonly  = CiR :whitespace() CiE CiA CiD CiO CiN CiL CiY :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
ExtName     = [ "\\" ] Id { "\\" Id } ;
ImplList    = ImplName { "," ImplName } ;
ImplName    <~~ push({impl: lastSeg(stripWs(up.in)), raw: stripWs(up.in)}) ~~> = ExtName ;
Member      = MethodDecl | ClassConst | UseTrait | PropDecl ;
// A method: of its modifiers only 'static' and the visibility are kept, and a reference-return
// '&' is accepted and ignored; an abstract/interface method has no body (';'
// instead of a block -> empty method).
MethodDecl  <~~ push({method: makeFun(takeAll())}) ~~>
            = [ AttrGrp ] { PropMod } KwFunction [ "&" ] MethodName <~~ push(up.in); genPush(); fnEnter(); cloEnterFn(up.in, true) ~~> ParamList [ ReturnType ] ( Block | ";" ) ;
// Class constants: const A = 1, B = 2; with an optional TYPE before the first name
// (the typed class constants of PHP 8.3). The typed form has to be its own
// alternative and be tried FIRST - writing it as an optional [ TypeHint ] would let
// 'const SIZE = 4' consume SIZE as the type and then fail on '=', because a PEG
// never retries an optional that matched.
ClassConst  <~~ push({cconsts: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = [ AttrGrp ] { PropMod } KwConst ( ConstTyped | ConstPlain ) ";" ;
ConstTyped  = TypeHint ConstItem { "," ConstItem } ;
ConstPlain  = ConstItem { "," ConstItem } ;
ConstItem   <~~ push({cconst: makeConstRec(takeAll())}) ~~>
            = ConstName [ PlainEq Expression ] ;
ConstName   <~~ push(up.in) ~~> = Id ;
// use T1, T2; inside a class body - and, optionally, the conflict-resolution block
// PHP spells with 'insteadof' (which of two traits wins a name) and 'as' (bind a
// trait method under a second name, and/or change its visibility).
UseTrait    <~~ push({usetrait: makeUseTrait(takeAll())}) ~~>
            = KwUse ImplList ( ";" | TraitAdapt ) ;
TraitAdapt  = "{" { TraitRule } "}" ;
TraitRule   = TraitInsteadof | TraitAs ;
TraitInsteadof <~~ push({tinsteadof: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = TraitRef KwInsteadof ImplList ";" ;
TraitAs     <~~ push({tas: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = TraitRef KwAs [ TraitVis ] [ TraitAlias ] ";" ;
// The qualified form has to come first: 'T::m' starts exactly like the bare 'm'.
TraitRef    = TraitQual | TraitMeth ;
TraitQual   = TraitOwner "::" TraitMeth ;
TraitOwner  <~~ push({towner: stripWs(up.in)}) ~~> = Name ;
TraitMeth   <~~ push({tmeth: stripWs(up.in)}) ~~> = MemberName ;
TraitVis    <~~ push({tvis: stripWs(up.in)}) ~~>
            = ( "public" | "private" | "protected" ) KwEnd ;
TraitAlias  <~~ push({talias: stripWs(up.in)}) ~~> = MemberName ;

// An interface is a named type with (abstract) method signatures: no bodies to run,
// but instanceof and the implements list need the name to exist at run time.
TypeDecl    = InterfaceDecl | TraitDecl ;
InterfaceDecl <~~ push(makeIfaceStmt(takeAll())) ~~>
            = [ AttrGrp ] KwInterface Name <~~ push(cloNoteClass(up.in)) ~~> [ KwExtends ImplList ] "{" { Member } "}" ;
// A trait is a bag of members to be COPIED into a class (it has no instances and no
// place in the inheritance chain), so its descriptor just carries its parsed parts.
TraitDecl   <~~ push(makeTraitStmt(takeAll())) ~~>
            = [ AttrGrp ] KwTrait Name <~~ push(cloNoteClass(up.in)) ~~> "{" { Member } "}" ;
// namespace N; and the BRACED form namespace N { ... } (and the global 'namespace {}').
// Names are not qualified in this subset, so the declaration itself stays a no-op -
// but the braced body is a top level of its own and its items are simply run in
// place, which is what makes a file that wraps everything in 'namespace X { }' work.
NsDecl      <~~ push(makeNs(takeAll(), up.pos)) ~~>
            = KwNamespace { Id | "\\" } ( Semi | NsBody ) ;
NsBody      <~~ push({nsbody: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = "{" { TopItem } "}" ;
// use A\B; / use A\B as C, D\E as F; / use function f; / use const C;
// and the GROUP form 'use Foo\Bar\{A, B as C, function f, const K,};' of PHP 7.
// Imports are not modelled in this subset, so the whole declaration is recognized
// structurally and routed to notImplemented - but it has to be recognized WHOLE, or
// the rest of the file is lost. UseGroup is tried first: its prefix ends in the '\{'
// that a plain path can never contain.
UseDecl     <~~ push(notImplStmt("use import", up.pos)) ~~>
            = KwUse [ UseKind ] UseItem { "," UseItem } Semi ;
UseKind     = ( "function" | "const" ) KwEnd ;
UseItem     = UseGroup | UsePath ;
UseGroup    = [ "\\" ] Id { "\\" Id } "\\" "{" UseInner { "," UseInner } [ "," ] "}" ;
UseInner    = [ UseKind ] UsePath ;
UsePath     = [ "\\" ] Id { "\\" Id } [ KwAs Id ] ;
// enum E: string implements I { case A = "a"; const C = 1; methods } - an enum is a
// class whose cases are SINGLETON instances, published as class constants (which is
// why E::A works with no extra machinery) and carrying ->name and, for a backed
// enum, ->value. EnumCase has to precede MethodDecl: both can start at a modifier.
EnumDecl    <~~ push(makeEnumStmt(takeAll())) ~~>
            = [ AttrGrp ] KwEnum Name <~~ push(cloNoteClass(up.in)) ~~> [ ":" TypeAtom ] [ KwImplements ImplList ] "{" { EnumMember } "}" ;
EnumMember  = EnumCase | MethodDecl | ClassConst | UseTrait ;
EnumCase    <~~ push({ecase: makeConstRec(takeAll())}) ~~>
            = [ AttrGrp ] KwCase ConstName [ PlainEq Expression ] ";" ;
SkipDq      = '"' :whitespace() { SkipStrEsc | SkipDqChar } '"' :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
SkipSq      = "'" :whitespace() { SkipStrEsc | SkipSqChar } "'" :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
SkipStrEsc  = "\\" "\x00"..."\U0010ffff" ;
SkipDqChar  = "\x00"..."\x21" | "\x23"..."\x5b" | "\x5d"..."\U0010ffff" ;
SkipSqChar  = "\x00"..."\x26" | "\x28"..."\x5b" | "\x5d"..."\U0010ffff" ;
// A property declaration 'public $x;' or 'public int $x = e;'. The default is
// replayed onto every new instance (initProps); the type decides whether a property
// without one starts null or uninitialised (PropType).
// One declaration may introduce SEVERAL properties - 'public $name, $bars;' and
// 'static public $a, $aa;' both occur in php-src's suite - so the tag pushes one
// {propdecl} per name rather than exactly one, and every name inherits the shared
// modifiers and type hint. makeClassStmt already walks the member list, so N records
// need no change there.
PropDecl    <~~ var pds = makeProps(takeAll()); for (var pi = 0; pi < pds.length; pi++) { push({propdecl: pds[pi]}) } ~~>
            = [ AttrGrp ] { PropMod } [ PropType ] DollarName <~~ cloNoteProp(up.in) ~~> [ "=" Expression ]
              { "," DollarName [ "=" Expression ] } ( ";" | PropHooks ) ;
// PHP 8.4 property hooks: 'public int $x = 1 { get => e; set { ... } }' (and the
// bodyless 'get;' / 'set;' of an abstract or interface property, which declares a hook
// without providing one). Each hook becomes an ordinary METHOD on the class -
// __hget_<prop> / __hset_<prop> - and a read or write of that property dispatches to
// it. The property itself stays a declared property and IS the backing store: inside
// its own hook '$this->x' reads and writes that store directly instead of re-entering
// the hook, which is PHP's rule and the only thing that keeps a backed hook from
// recursing forever. Twin of PropHooks in php-to-llvm-ir.abnf.
PropHooks   <~~ push({hooks: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = "{" { PropHook } "}" ;
PropHook    <~~ push(makeHook(takeAll())) ~~>
            = [ AttrGrp ] { PropMod } [ "&" ] HookName <~~ push({hname: cloEnterHook(stripWs(up.in))}) ~~>
              ( HookArrow | HookBlock | ";" ) ;
HookName    = ( "get" | "set" ) KwEnd ;
HookArrow   <~~ push({harrow: pop()}) ~~> = [ ParamList ] "=>" Expression ";" ;
HookBlock   <~~ push({hblock: pop().body}) ~~> = [ ParamList ] Block ;

// 'private(set)' / 'protected(set)' is PHP 8.4 asymmetric visibility: the setter is
// more restricted than the getter. Only the plain visibility is enforced; the
// '(set)' suffix is accepted and leaves writes at that visibility.
Modifier    = ( "public" | "private" | "protected" | "static" | "final" | "abstract" | "readonly" ) KwEnd [ SetVis ] ;
SetVis      = "(" "set" ")" ;
// The modifier text is CAPTURED (as {mod}) rather than dropped: 'static' decides
// whether a property lives on the class or on the instance, and any modifier on a
// constructor parameter makes it a PROMOTED property.
PropMod     <~~ push({mod: stripWs(up.in)}) ~~> = Modifier ;
// A property's declared type. A typed property without a default starts
// UNINITIALISED - its slot holds undefined, not null - and var_dump names the type
// (uninitialized(?Foo\C)), so the text is kept.
PropType    <~~ push({ptype: propTypeText(up.in)}) ~~> = TypeHint ;

// Body of a control structure / function: braces around a statement sequence.
Block       <~~ push({body: makeBody(takeAll())}) ~~>
            = "{" { Statement } "}" ;
// A bare '{ ... }' statement: the block's body runs in place (PHP has no block scope).
BlockStmt   <~~ push(pop().body) ~~> = Block ;

// Every statement pushes exactly one thunk: function() -> value | signal.
// A declaration is a statement too: PHP allows `if (...) { class Foo {} }` and a
// function declared inside a function body, and a conditionally declared class is
// common enough in real files to be worth parsing. DefStmt has to wrap Def so the thunk it
// pushes is the same makeDefStmt record TopItem builds; an ANONYMOUS function still
// rolls back here (Def demands a Name) and reaches ClosureExpr through ExprStmt.
Statement   = InlineHtml | ShortEcho | EchoStmt | UnsetStmt | GlobalStmt | StaticVarStmt | BlockStmt | If | While | ForEach | For
            | Switch | DoWhile | Try | Throw | Declare | Goto
            | Return | Break | Continue
            | DefStmt | ClassDecl | EnumDecl | TypeDecl | ConstDecl
            | Empty | LabelStmt | ExprStmt ;
DefStmt     <~~ push(makeDefStmt(pop())) ~~> = Def ;

// ----- switch, do-while, declare, goto, static locals -----
// static $n = 0; inside a function: a variable whose STORAGE outlives the call. The
// cell is created on the first execution of this very statement and re-bound into
// every later frame, which is exactly PHP's per-declaration (not per-object) rule.
StaticVarStmt <~~ push(makeStaticVars(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwStatic DollarName [ PlainEq Expression ] { "," DollarName [ PlainEq Expression ] } Semi ;
// switch: loose (==) comparison, first match wins and execution FALLS THROUGH into
// the following clauses until a break. 'default' may sit anywhere, and is only taken
// when no case matched. The clause bodies are plain statement runs, which is why they
// are collected as lists rather than as Blocks - a Block would give each its own
// scope and would stop the fallthrough.
Switch      <~~ loopLeave(); push(makeSwitch(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwSwitch <~~ loopEnter() ~~> "(" Expression ")" ( SwitchAlt | SwitchBlock ) ;
SwitchBlock = "{" [ ";" ] { SwitchClause } "}" ;
SwitchAlt   = ":" [ ";" ] { SwitchClause } KwEndswitch Semi ;
SwitchClause = CaseClause | DefaultClause ;
CaseClause  <~~ push({scase: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = KwCase Expression CaseSep { Statement } ;
DefaultClause <~~ push({sdefault: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = KwDefault CaseSep { Statement } ;
CaseSep     = ":" !":" | ";" ;
DoWhile     <~~ loopLeave(); push(makeDoWhile(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwDo <~~ loopEnter() ~~> Body KwWhile "(" Expression ")" Semi ;
// goto L; / L: - a jump within one statement sequence (which is all PHP allows: it
// cannot enter a loop or leave a function). The jump travels as a control signal and
// is caught by the statement list that owns the label; see makeBody.
Goto        <~~ push(makeGoto(pop())) ~~>
            = KwGoto LabelName Semi ;
LabelStmt   <~~ push(makeLabel(pop())) ~~>
            = LabelName ":" !":" ;
LabelName   <~~ push(stripWs(up.in)) ~~> = Name ;
